      "rev_n_1_cf889a_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_cf889a_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_cf889a",
        "thought_text": "I apologize for the oversight. The error in the previous reasoning was that it didn't consider the possibility of multiple rotations for each gear. Let's refine the thought process to address this issue:\n\nInstead of simulating a single rotation for each gear, we should consider all possible combinations of rotations for each gear. This means we need to check all 2^n possible rotation states for each gear. We can represent each gear's state as a binary number (0 for no rotation, 1 for clockwise rotation), and then iterate through all possible combinations of these states.\n\nThis approach will ensure that we catch all possible scenarios, including multiple rotations of the same gear, which is crucial for determining whether the puzzle is real or fake.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_cf889a_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_3c72d758a8",
        "created_at": 1794530223.405161
      },
      "rev_n_3_9fba7e_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_9fba7e_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_9fba7e",
        "thought_text": "The error in the original code is that it only checks for a single starting point, which may not be sufficient to determine if the puzzle is real. We need to consider all possible rotations of the gears, not just a single starting point. This means we should check if the sequence can be transformed into 0, 1, 2, ..., n-1 for any rotation of the gears.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_9fba7e_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_5b42ac1748",
